{
  "description": "Answer cache keys and principals (section 7.3). Each case is one read through the cache. Build the client principalKind names from each credential exactly as given, before it is trimmed: for key, the server client from a secret key in credential, or the key-only in-app client from a publishable key (one starting ent_pk_ once trimmed); for customer-token, the in-app client from the customer token in credential; for identity, the in-app client from key and identityToken. Give it built.baseUrl and, for an in-app client, visitor, or clientVisitor when that is present. Make the read built.route names with built.params: /customers/{id}/entitlements/{feature} is check(feature), /customers/{id}/entitlements is entitlements(), /customers/{id}/plans is plans(), /customers/{id}/pricing is the customer's pricing(), /pricing is the client's pricing() and /pricing/features is features(); {id} is the literal me on the in-app client. A read is given visitor on the server client when it is not null, and on an in-app client only when clientVisitor is present; a server customer's check is given asOf when it is not null, as asOfInput when that is present (an instant with an offset, which the SDK formats as asOf). On the server client, give it a custom cache store and compare the key the store is asked for with cacheKey. In-app clients take no custom store (sections 4.3 and 7.3), so on them compare the key their in-memory store is asked for, read through the unit suite's own access to the SDK's internals, never through a public option. Compare the request's Authorization, Entitler-Identity-Token, Entitler-As-Of and Entitler-Visitor headers with requestHeaders, where null means the header is not sent. principal is the lowercase hex SHA-256 of the trimmed credential, or of the trimmed key, a line feed and the trimmed identity token, and serialised is the exact JSON array text whose SHA-256 is cacheKey. A case with appliesWhere runs only where that holds: an in-app read can be given asOf only where one options type serves both clients' reads, and such a case gives asOfInput to the in-app check, which fails before any request with outcome's argument error, so no key is asked for.",
